export const pkUnit07CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 7: Move and Play — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 7: Move and Play helps children connect whole-body movement, playground routines, ball play, rhythm, simple obstacle routes, group games, and safe social participation. Compared with PG, PK children can follow clearer action cues, participate in short turn-taking games, respond to go/stop signals more consistently, and attempt simple action words or short chunks when ready.\n\nThis unit remains non-language developmental work and should not become a formal sports-skills unit. Children are not expected to perform standard PE techniques, compete, keep score, or explain movement rules. The focus is safe body movement, gross-motor confidence, action response, self-regulation, turn-taking, and joyful group play.\n\nThe core PK movement focus includes `go ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ play ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow`. Children may use short chunks such as `my turn`, `go`, `stop`, `roll the ball`, `kick the ball`, or `all done` when ready.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\ngo ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ play ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow ｜ ball ｜ beanbag ｜ hoop ｜ tunnel ｜ mat ｜ friend ｜ turn ｜ wait ｜ safe body\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nGo. ｜ Stop. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Run safely. ｜ Jump on the mat. ｜ Hop here. ｜ Dance with the scarf. ｜ Crawl through the tunnel. ｜ Climb up. ｜ Come down. ｜ Roll the ball. ｜ Throw the beanbag. ｜ Catch the ball. ｜ Kick the ball. ｜ Push it. ｜ Pull it. ｜ Fast or slow? ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ My turn. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Go, Stop, Walk and Run\n\nChildren strengthen safe movement response through go/stop cues, walking paths, running zones, floor spots, music cues, and visual signs. The focus is controlled movement and stopping.\n\n### Week 2: Jump, Hop, Dance, Up and Down\n\nChildren explore body coordination through jumping, hopping, dancing, climbing low safe structures, going up/down, and moving with scarves, mats, and rhythm.\n\n### Week 3: Ball and Beanbag Play\n\nChildren practise rolling, throwing soft objects, catching with support, kicking, passing, waiting, and taking turns with balls and beanbags.\n\n### Week 4: Obstacle Route and Group Play\n\nChildren integrate movement actions through tunnels, hoops, mats, push/pull objects, obstacle routes, simple partner play, group clean-up, and safe transition routines.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Movement Input\n\ngo ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow ｜ safe body ｜ come here ｜ wait\n\n### Ball and Object Input\n\nball ｜ beanbag ｜ hoop ｜ tunnel ｜ mat ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ pass ｜ basket ｜ clean up\n\n### Social and Routine Input\n\nplay ｜ friend ｜ turn ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ wait ｜ together ｜ stop ｜ start ｜ line up ｜ all done ｜ good job ｜ help\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nGo. ｜ Stop. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Run safely. ｜ Jump on the mat. ｜ Hop here. ｜ Crawl through the tunnel. ｜ Roll the ball. ｜ Throw the beanbag. ｜ Catch the ball. ｜ Kick the ball. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Come here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by walking, running in a safe area, stopping on cue, jumping on mats, hopping, dancing, crawling through tunnels, climbing low safe equipment with support, moving up and down, rolling balls, throwing beanbags, catching with adult help, kicking balls, pushing or pulling soft objects, waiting for turns, passing objects, following a short obstacle route, returning materials, and using short movement or social chunks when ready.\n\nChildren are not expected to perform sports techniques accurately, follow complex game rules, compete, keep score, explain safety verbally, or use full movement sentences.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nWalking Walking  \nCount & Move  \nLet's Go To The Zoo  \nHello Hello! Can You Clap Your Hands  \nOpen Shut Them  \nThe Pinocchio  \nWalking In The Jungle  \nPass the Beanbag  \nWe All Fall Down  \nHead Shoulders Knees & Toes  \nThe Hokey Pokey Shake  \nIf You're Happy\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should support go/stop routines, walking, running, jumping, hopping, dancing, body actions, passing, balancing, falling/resting safely, and group rhythm. Children may sing repeated action words when ready, but movement response, gesture, rhythm, and safe participation remain valid.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nIndoor physical fitness area  \nOutdoor grass area  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nLarge climbing / playground area  \nSandpit  \nClassroom mat area  \nMusic classroom  \nTheatre / movement space  \nSoft block area  \nBasketball / tennis court when simplified and safe\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nSoft balls  \nBeanbags  \nScarves  \nMats  \nHoops  \nFloor spots  \nFootprint markers  \nCones  \nTunnels  \nSoft blocks  \nLarge foam blocks  \nPush carts  \nPull ropes  \nFabric bands  \nParachute cloth  \nBaskets  \nLow platforms  \nBalance paths  \nVisual go/stop cards  \nWalking rope / rings  \nSimple obstacle-route cards\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nThe indoor fitness area can support obstacle routes, tunnels, mats, and safe gross-motor play. Outdoor grass can support running, rolling, throwing, and kicking with clear boundaries. Soft balls and beanbags are preferred over hard sports balls. Cones, floor spots, footprint markers, and go/stop cards help PK children understand movement expectations visually.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a PE assessment unit. The focus is movement participation, not technical skill.\n\nThis unit should not use competitive games, elimination, scoring, or complex rules.\n\nThis unit should not require all children to say action words. Body response and safe participation are core outcomes.\n\nThis unit should manage safety through soft materials, visible boundaries, short routes, adult proximity, and clear stop cues.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children respond to go/stop cues, move with increasing body awareness, join walking/running/jumping/hopping/dancing routines, follow simple obstacle routes, handle balls and beanbags safely, wait and take turns, participate with peers, respond to fast/slow and up/down cues, clean up movement materials, and use short action chunks when ready.\n\nObservation should focus on gross-motor confidence, action comprehension, safe self-regulation, turn-taking, peer awareness, and joyful participation rather than sports performance or verbal mastery.\n",
  "zh": "# PK Unit 7: Move and Play — Common Info｜PK 单元 7：动起来与游戏 — 通用信息\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview｜单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 7：动起来与游戏帮助儿童把全身动作、操场常规、球类游戏、节奏、简单的障碍路线、集体游戏和安全的社交参与连接起来。和 PG 相比，PK 儿童可以跟随更清晰的动作提示、参与简短的轮流游戏、更一致地回应走／停信号，以及在准备好时尝试简单的动作词或简短句块。\n\n本单元仍是非语言发展工作，不应变成正式的体育技能单元。不要求儿童做标准的体育技巧、竞争、计分或解释动作规则。重点是安全的身体动作、大肌肉信心、动作反应、自我调节、轮流和愉快的集体游戏。\n\n核心 PK 动作聚焦包括 `go ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ play ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow`。儿童在准备好时可以使用简短句块，例如 `my turn`、`go`、`stop`、`roll the ball`、`kick the ball`、`all done`。\n\n## 2. Unit Language｜单元语言\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary｜PK 核心词汇\n\ngo ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ play ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow ｜ ball ｜ beanbag ｜ hoop ｜ tunnel ｜ mat ｜ friend ｜ turn ｜ wait ｜ safe body\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks｜实用教师句块\n\nGo. ｜ Stop. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Run safely. ｜ Jump on the mat. ｜ Hop here. ｜ Dance with the scarf. ｜ Crawl through the tunnel. ｜ Climb up. ｜ Come down. ｜ Roll the ball. ｜ Throw the beanbag. ｜ Catch the ball. ｜ Kick the ball. ｜ Push it. ｜ Pull it. ｜ Fast or slow? ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ My turn. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Clean up.\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes｜周次子主题\n\n### Week 1: Go, Stop, Walk and Run｜第一周：走、停、走和跑\n\n儿童通过走／停提示、行走路径、跑区、地面标记、音乐提示和视觉标志来强化安全动作反应。重点是受控动作和停下来。\n\n### Week 2: Jump, Hop, Dance, Up and Down｜第二周：跳、单脚跳、舞蹈、向上和向下\n\n儿童通过跳跃、单脚跳、舞蹈、攀爬低矮安全结构、上／下移动以及与丝巾、垫子和节奏一起移动来探索身体协调。\n\n### Week 3: Ball and Beanbag Play｜第三周：球与豆袋游戏\n\n儿童练习滚动、扔软物、在支持下接球、踢、传递、等待，以及与球和豆袋轮流。\n\n### Week 4: Obstacle Route and Group Play｜第四周：障碍路线和集体游戏\n\n儿童通过隧道、呼啦圈、垫子、推／拉物品、障碍路线、简单的搭档游戏、集体收拾和安全过渡常规把动作整合起来。\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool｜教师输入库\n\n### Movement Input｜动作输入\n\ngo ｜ stop ｜ walk ｜ run ｜ jump ｜ hop ｜ dance ｜ climb ｜ crawl ｜ up ｜ down ｜ fast ｜ slow ｜ safe body ｜ come here ｜ wait\n\n### Ball and Object Input｜球与物品输入\n\nball ｜ beanbag ｜ hoop ｜ tunnel ｜ mat ｜ roll ｜ throw ｜ catch ｜ kick ｜ push ｜ pull ｜ pass ｜ basket ｜ clean up\n\n### Social and Routine Input｜社交与常规输入\n\nplay ｜ friend ｜ turn ｜ your turn ｜ my turn ｜ wait ｜ together ｜ stop ｜ start ｜ line up ｜ all done ｜ good job ｜ help\n\n### Teacher Routine Language｜教师常规语言\n\nGo. ｜ Stop. ｜ Walk slowly. ｜ Run safely. ｜ Jump on the mat. ｜ Hop here. ｜ Crawl through the tunnel. ｜ Roll the ball. ｜ Throw the beanbag. ｜ Catch the ball. ｜ Kick the ball. ｜ Wait. ｜ Your turn. ｜ Safe body. ｜ Come here. ｜ Clean up. ｜ All done. ｜ Good job.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes｜预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：走、在安全区域跑、按提示停下、在垫子上跳、单脚跳、跳舞、爬过隧道、在支持下攀爬低矮安全设备、上和下移动、滚球、扔豆袋、在成人帮助下接球、踢球、推或拉软物、等待轮流、传递物品、跟随一条简短的障碍路线、归还材料，以及在准备好时使用简短动作或社交句块。\n\n不要求儿童准确地做体育技巧、跟随复杂的游戏规则、竞争、计分、口头解释安全，或使用完整动作句。\n\n## 6. Core Unit Songs｜单元核心歌曲\n\n### Core Songs｜核心歌曲\n\nWalking Walking  \nCount & Move  \nLet's Go To The Zoo  \nHello Hello! Can You Clap Your Hands  \nOpen Shut Them  \nThe Pinocchio  \nWalking In The Jungle  \nPass the Beanbag  \nWe All Fall Down  \nHead Shoulders Knees & Toes  \nThe Hokey Pokey Shake  \nIf You're Happy\n\n### Use of Songs｜歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当支持走／停常规、行走、跑、跳、单脚跳、舞蹈、身体动作、传递、平衡、安全倒下／休息和集体节奏。儿童在准备好时可以唱重复的动作词，但动作反应、手势、节奏和安全参与仍有效。\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities｜资源机会\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces｜可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nIndoor physical fitness area  \nOutdoor grass area  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nLarge climbing / playground area  \nSandpit  \nClassroom mat area  \nMusic classroom  \nTheatre / movement space  \nSoft block area  \nBasketball / tennis court when simplified and safe\n\n### Useful Materials｜可用材料\n\nSoft balls  \nBeanbags  \nScarves  \nMats  \nHoops  \nFloor spots  \nFootprint markers  \nCones  \nTunnels  \nSoft blocks  \nLarge foam blocks  \nPush carts  \nPull ropes  \nFabric bands  \nParachute cloth  \nBaskets  \nLow platforms  \nBalance paths  \nVisual go/stop cards  \nWalking rope / rings  \nSimple obstacle-route cards\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities｜拓展资源机会\n\n室内健身区可以支持障碍路线、隧道、垫子和安全的大肌肉游戏。户外草地可以在边界清晰的情况下支持跑、滚、扔和踢。软球和豆袋比硬质运动球更合适。锥筒、地面标记、脚印标记和走／停卡片帮助 PK 儿童视觉上理解动作预期。\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes｜边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成体育评估单元。重点是动作参与，而非技术技能。\n\n本单元不应使用竞争游戏、淘汰、计分或复杂规则。\n\n本单元不应要求所有儿童说动作词。身体反应和安全参与是核心结果。\n\n本单元应通过软材料、可见的边界、短路线、成人接近和清晰的停提示来管理安全。\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus｜单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否回应走／停提示、动作时身体意识增强、加入走／跑／跳／单脚跳／舞的常规、跟随简单的障碍路线、安全处理球和豆袋、等待和轮流、和同伴一起参与、回应快／慢和上／下提示、收拾动作材料，以及在准备好时使用简短动作句块。\n\n观察应聚焦于大肌肉信心、动作理解、安全自我调节、轮流、同伴意识和愉快的参与，而非体育表现或口头掌握。"
};

